From d71edc1c7cfcd84080e60365cfdc3dee97918578 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:37:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] prose_chip: three chips, nine voices, and this machine sends
 the notation

A virtio device (ID 60) with one control queue, and a BChipPlayer over it.
A program hands over ABC source -- a few hundred bytes -- and the host
parses it, schedules it against the Mac's own sample clock and makes the
sound. There is no audio in this machine at all: no samples, no mixing,
no buffer to keep fed. A second of stereo is four hundred kilobytes where
a tune is four hundred bytes, and the clock that drives a speaker is not
this one's.

Where a tune goes is the notation's own business. `[I:chip ...]` is for
the three-chip synthesiser; `%%MIDI program n` says it was written for a
synthesiser and it goes to the Mac's General MIDI one. Neither the
program nor the kit chooses.

Four tracks share the nine voices, so a tune and a sound effect are not
the same schedule. Which voices each uses is the tune's business: the
grammar says `v=` explicitly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Prose-Patch: 0115-prose-chip-three-chips-nine-voices-and-this-machine-sends-th.patch b4719e1bdc0975e6471f47ebaf7732b1e9c0b2915e7053fe3e80f51c3c2f74eb
